%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter 8 text_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 93f6bf3b27ccfbe489a979e40cca16e1, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 111713996800
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave><color=yellow>Welcome aboard!</color></anim> Charlie
      here. This processor likes to get things done: <color=orange>one instruction,
      one clock cycle</color>. First up: <color=#48DBFB>addi x6, x6, 4</color> at
      address 0.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 140197515264
    m_Localized: Instruction Memory is read at the <color=yellow>PC</color>. The
      Register File supplies x6, the Extender supplies 4, and the ALU adds them.
      The result can travel all the way back to x6 <color=orange>within the same
      cycle</color>. No Instruction Register or ALUOut register needs an extra clock
      here.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 150624555008
    m_Localized: We start with <color=yellow>PC = 0</color> and <color=yellow>x6
      = 8</color>. Your job is to write <color=green>12 into x6</color>. Data Memory
      starts with 1, 2, 3, 4 at addresses 0, 4, 8, 12; ADDI must leave it alone.
      The normal next PC is 4.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 160497946624
    m_Localized: 'All right, control-room captain! <color=yellow>What would help
      you now?</color> Set up the whole path before the clock: the moving signals
      show parts of <color=#48DBFB>one cycle</color>, not separate processor cycles.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183191941894144
    m_Localized: 'Execute ADDI at address 0 in one cycle: x6 = 8 + 4 = 12.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183264188780544
    m_Localized: 'Execute ADD at address 0 in one cycle: x4 = x1 + x3 = 44.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183290549981184
    m_Localized: 'Reach address 8, then execute ORI in one cycle: x12 OR 25 = 63.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183300763111424
    m_Localized: 'Execute SW at address 0: store x4 = 5 in Data Memory[4].'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183309646647296
    m_Localized: 'BEQ at address 12: x1 != x3, so continue to PC = 16 in one cycle.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2183319201271808
    m_Localized: 'JAL at address 12: x1 = 16. Goal PC = 256 conflicts with result
      268; see Charlie''s hint.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483477852160
    m_Localized: 'All right, control-room captain! <color=yellow>What would help
      you now?</color> Set up the whole path before the clock: the moving signals
      show parts of <color=#48DBFB>one cycle</color>, not separate processor cycles.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904064
    m_Localized: I'm ready!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904065
    m_Localized: Explain the data path.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904066
    m_Localized: Show me the control signals.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904067
    m_Localized: Back to my choices.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904068
    m_Localized: One more hint, please.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904069
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Your turn! You've got this.</color></anim>
      Click the blocks to inspect their panels, choose the control settings, then
      advance the clock. Use the back arrow if you need to undo a step. <color=orange>Check</color>
      tests the task's stored goal.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904070
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>Charlie to the rescue!</anim> Which part of the <color=orange>single-cycle
      processor</color> is giving you trouble? Pick your instruction. These hints
      never advance the clock for you.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904071
    m_Localized: Arithmetic / logic
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904072
    m_Localized: Store to memory
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904073
    m_Localized: Branch / jump
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904074
    m_Localized: <color=orange>ADDI</color> adds an immediate, <color=orange>ADD</color>
      adds two registers, and <color=orange>ORI</color> combines bits. Which one
      are you working on?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904075
    m_Localized: Only <color=orange>SW</color> belongs to the memory exercise in
      this chapter. Instruction Memory supplies the instruction; Data Memory receives
      the stored value. They are separate blocks.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904076
    m_Localized: <color=orange>BEQ</color> depends on a comparison. <color=orange>JAL</color>
      jumps and saves a return address. Here you choose the PC MUX yourself; there
      is no automatic branch selection.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904077
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Back to the controls!</color></anim> Check
      your MUX choices and write enables before advancing the clock. You can reopen
      this hint menu whenever you need it.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904078
    m_Localized: Back to the processor.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904079
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave><color=yellow>Welcome aboard!</color></anim> Charlie
      here. This processor likes to get things done: <color=orange>one instruction,
      one clock cycle</color>. First up: <color=#48DBFB>addi x6, x6, 4</color> at
      address 0.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904080
    m_Localized: Instruction Memory is read at the <color=yellow>PC</color>. The
      Register File supplies x6, the Extender supplies 4, and the ALU adds them.
      The result can travel all the way back to x6 <color=orange>within the same
      cycle</color>. No Instruction Register or ALUOut register needs an extra clock
      here.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904081
    m_Localized: We start with <color=yellow>PC = 0</color> and <color=yellow>x6
      = 8</color>. Your job is to write <color=green>12 into x6</color>. Data Memory
      starts with 1, 2, 3, 4 at addresses 0, 4, 8, 12; ADDI must leave it alone.
      The normal next PC is 4.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904082
    m_Localized: The <color=orange>I-format immediate</color> is the number 4, not
      a second register address. So SrcB must come from the Extender. Result must
      come from the main ALU. PC+4 has its own ALU in this scene, so you do not have
      to borrow the main ALU to advance the PC.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904083
    m_Localized: 'Control recipe: <color=orange>Extender I; SrcB MUX 1 (immediate);
      main ALU ADD; Result MUX 0 (ALU)</color>. Enable Register File writing. Disable
      Data Memory writing. For normal flow choose PC ALU ADD, PC MUX 0 (PC+4), PC
      writing enabled. MUX numbers are the displayed 0-based input labels. Set everything
      first, then use <color=#48DBFB>one clock</color>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904084
    m_Localized: <anim:shake>No clock-by-clock assembly line this time!</anim> The
      long signal animation still belongs to a single clock action. If x6 does not
      change, inspect Register File write enable and Result MUX before trying again.
      Don't keep clocking into the zero words after address 0.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904085
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>Two registers walk into an ALU...</anim> No punchline,
      just <color=orange>ADD</color>! At address 0 we have <color=#48DBFB>add x4,
      x1, x3</color>. Let's bring both operands together in one cycle.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904086
    m_Localized: An <color=orange>R-type instruction</color> supplies two source
      register addresses and a destination address. RD1 goes straight to the main
      ALU. RD2 must pass through SrcB MUX. The ALU result returns through Result
      MUX to the destination, all before the same clock edge updates the registers.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904087
    m_Localized: '<color=yellow>PC = 0, x1 = 1, x3 = 43</color>. Replace the initial
      x4 = 5 with <color=green>44</color>. Keep Data Memory unchanged: its values
      are 12, 16, -16, -12. One instruction needs one execution cycle; normal PC+4
      gives PC = 4.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904088
    m_Localized: Unlike ADDI, <color=orange>ADD has no immediate operand</color>.
      Selecting Extender output here would feed instruction bits into the addition
      instead of x3. The Extender setting does not affect the result when SrcB selects
      RD2 and PC selects PC+4.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904089
    m_Localized: Choose <color=orange>SrcB MUX 0 (RD2), main ALU ADD, Result MUX
      0 (ALU)</color>. Enable Register File writing; disable Data Memory writing.
      Choose PC ALU ADD and PC MUX 0, with PC writing enabled. Then one clock writes
      1 + 43 to x4. The destination comes from the instruction; you do not select
      x4 manually.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904090
    m_Localized: "Think <color=#48DBFB>Register File \u2192 ALU \u2192 Register File</color>.
      Data Memory is not a stop on this route. Result MUX input 1 would select memory
      data, not the sum. <anim:wave>You've already met all the pieces!</anim>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904091
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>Time for a little bit magic!</anim> The instruction <color=#48DBFB>ori
      x12, x12, 25</color> waits at address 8, but <color=yellow>PC starts at 0</color>.
      First find the right instruction, then execute it in one cycle.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904092
    m_Localized: '<color=orange>ORI</color> combines corresponding bits with logical
      OR. It does not add 25! The I-format immediate goes through SrcB MUX to the
      main ALU. In this setup, <color=yellow>63 OR 25 = 63</color>: every set bit
      of 25 is already set in 63.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904093
    m_Localized: At addresses 0 and 4 there are <color=yellow>zero words, not the
      ORI instruction</color>. Keep Register File and Data Memory writing disabled
      while two PC+4 clock actions move PC from 0 to 4 to 8. Then execute the instruction
      at 8 in one cycle. These two navigation steps are not extra execution phases
      of ORI.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904094
    m_Localized: The binary operation is <color=#48DBFB>111111 OR 011001 = 111111</color>.
      Select OR, not ADD. The stored goal only checks x12 = 63, which is already
      true initially. That check alone does not demonstrate execution; follow the
      path to address 8 and inspect your control settings.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904095
    m_Localized: 'Preparation: PC ALU ADD, PC MUX 0, PC writing on; Register File
      and Data Memory writing off. Advance twice to PC = 8. For ORI: <color=orange>Extender
      I, SrcB MUX 1, main ALU OR, Result MUX 0</color>; enable Register File writing,
      keep Data Memory writing off. One further clock executes ORI and normally advances
      PC to 12.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904096
    m_Localized: <color=yellow>x12 stays 63 even when you do everything right.</color>
      That is expected for these starting values. A success message before execution
      is a limitation of this exercise's stored goal, not proof that the processor
      ran the instruction. Data Memory starts at zero and should stay unchanged.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904097
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave>Numbers deserve a home, too!</anim> This time <color=#48DBFB>sw
      x4, 1(x8)</color> at address 0 puts a value into <color=orange>Data Memory</color>.
      Instruction Memory keeps its own job: supplying the command.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904098
    m_Localized: SW uses <color=yellow>x8 as the base address</color> and <color=yellow>x4
      as the data</color>. The main ALU adds the S-format offset to x8. RD2 supplies
      x4 directly to Data Memory's write-data input. Address calculation and the
      store belong to <color=#48DBFB>one cycle</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904099
    m_Localized: We start at <color=yellow>PC = 0, x8 = 3, x4 = 5</color>. The effective
      address is 3 + 1 = <color=green>4</color>. Write <color=green>5 to Data Memory[4]</color>,
      initially zero. Do not write the address or result into a register. The other
      data words stay zero.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483586904100
    m_Localized: '<color=orange>SW has no destination register.</color> The bits
      that look like rd in other formats are part of the store offset. Keep Register
      File write enable off. Result MUX does not control the stored data: RD2 goes
      straight to Data Memory WD.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098368
    m_Localized: Choose <color=orange>Extender S, SrcB MUX 1 (immediate), main ALU
      ADD</color>. Turn Data Memory writing on and Register File writing off. For
      the next PC use PC ALU ADD, PC MUX 0 and PC writing on. Result MUX is irrelevant
      while Register File writing is off. One clock stores x4 = 5 at address 4.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098369
    m_Localized: Do not confuse <color=yellow>the address 4</color> with <color=yellow>the
      value 5</color>. The ALU computes the address; x4 supplies the value. There
      is no separate address-computation clock followed by a store clock in this
      processor. <anim:wave>One setup, one execution cycle!</anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098370
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave>Time for a decision!</anim> <color=#48DBFB>beq x1, x3,
      1028</color> waits at <color=yellow>PC = 12</color>. A branch is not a promise
      to jump: first ask whether its condition is true.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098371
    m_Localized: 'BEQ means <color=orange>branch if equal</color>. Subtracting the
      two source values tells us whether they are equal: zero means yes. In this
      scene, <color=yellow>you choose PC MUX yourself</color>; the computed zero
      flag does not automatically choose the next PC. Comparison and PC update still
      belong to one cycle.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098372
    m_Localized: <color=yellow>x1 = 1, x3 = 43</color>, so 1 - 43 = -42, not zero.
      This branch is <color=orange>not taken</color>. Choose the normal successor
      <color=green>PC = 12 + 4 = 16</color>. Leave Register File and Data Memory
      unchanged; the stored goal checks PC = 16.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098373
    m_Localized: With B-format extension, the offset is <color=yellow>1028</color>.
      A taken branch would use PC + offset = 1040, calculated by the separate BTA
      ALU. But these operands are unequal, so the correct route is PC+4. Register
      File writeback is not part of BEQ.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098374
    m_Localized: Set <color=orange>Extender B, SrcB MUX 0 (RD2), main ALU SUB</color>.
      Keep Register File and Data Memory writing off. Set PC ALU ADD, PC MUX 0 (PC+4),
      PC writing on. BTA ALU ADD computes the unused branch target. Result MUX is
      irrelevant here. One clock should advance PC to 16.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098375
    m_Localized: <anim:shake>BEQ does not mean always jump!</anim> The ALU result
      -42 is only the comparison result; do not write it into a register or memory.
      The PC MUX is manual in this teaching model, so base your choice on the comparison
      rather than expecting automatic branch control.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098376
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave>Jump now, return later!</anim> <color=orange>JAL</color>
      is useful for subroutines: it redirects the PC and saves a return address.
      Here <color=#48DBFB>jal x1, 256</color> sits at <color=yellow>PC = 12</color>.
      There is a configuration catch we need to discuss honestly.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098377
    m_Localized: 'JAL has two results in <color=#48DBFB>one execution cycle</color>:
      <color=yellow>rd = PC+4</color> and <color=yellow>next PC = PC + J-immediate</color>.
      Separate PC and BTA ALUs provide these values in parallel. Result MUX sends
      PC+4 to x1; PC MUX sends the target to PC.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098378
    m_Localized: 'The return address is <color=green>12 + 4 = 16</color>. But the
      J-immediate is a relative offset: 12 + 256 = <color=yellow>268</color>. This
      exercise''s unchanged stored goal asks for <color=orange>x1 = 16 and PC = 256</color>.
      Those PC values disagree; correct single-cycle execution cannot satisfy that
      stored goal.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098379
    m_Localized: <color=orange>256 is the offset, not an absolute target address.</color>
      Do not replace PC with the immediate directly or invent an extra cycle. Correct
      JAL adds the current instruction address. The expected link value 16 is consistent;
      only the stored target PC conflicts with the instruction.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483591098380
    m_Localized: 'For JAL: <color=orange>Extender J, PC ALU ADD, BTA ALU ADD, Result
      MUX 2 (PC+4), PC MUX 1 (BTA)</color>. PC and Register File writing on; Data
      Memory writing off. Main ALU and SrcB are irrelevant to the JAL results. One
      clock gives x1 = 16, PC = 268. Check instead requires PC = 256: a configuration
      mismatch.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599483599486976
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>You found the catch, not an addition mistake!</anim>
      These PC-MUX paths cannot produce PC = 256 from this instruction in one correct
      cycle. Use this task to inspect JAL until its configuration is corrected. A
      control-bit trick cannot replace a correct target.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668928
    m_Localized: addi x6, x6, 4
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668929
    m_Localized: add x4, x1, x3
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668930
    m_Localized: ori x12, x12, 25
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668931
    m_Localized: sw x4, 1(x8)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668932
    m_Localized: beq x1, x3, 1028
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 19599568588668933
    m_Localized: jal x1, 256
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
